------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

The GW31-E is a small Linux gateway that bridges battery sensors to LTE. It
also carries a Wi-Fi radio, and it periodically scans for access points: the
roaming logic wants to know which AP to associate with, the cloud agent
uploads the neighbourhood for site surveys, and the local CLI prints it for
installers. Several consumers, one radio.

The vendor driver gives you this and nothing else:

    struct ApInfo {
        uint8_t bssid[6];   /* MAC of the AP — the only stable identity   */
        char    ssid[33];   /* NUL-terminated, may be empty (hidden SSID) */
        int8_t  rssi_dbm;   /* signal strength, e.g. -42                  */
        uint8_t channel;
    };

    int wifi_scan(struct ApInfo *out, size_t max, size_t *found);

  - BLOCKS for the whole scan: 100-300 ms on real hardware. There is no
    "start scan / poll for result" pair. You get one blocking call.
  - Fills up to `max` entries and sets *found to how many APs were SEEN,
    0..40. The result length CHANGES on every scan: an empty scan (0 APs) is
    a legal answer, not an error.
  - *found may be LARGER than max. Only min(*found, max) entries were
    written; reading past that is on you.
  - Returns -1 if the radio is BUSY. On -1, `out` and `*found` are
    unspecified — do not read them. -1 comes back immediately, not after a
    delay.
  - NOT thread-safe (static internal state). Exactly ONE caller, ever.

    uint64_t wifi_now_us(void);   /* gettimeofday-based, NOT monotonic */

  Harness scaling: the fake radio in main.c blocks 40-120 ms instead of
  100-300 ms, and returns BUSY on every 7th call, so the whole run finishes
  in about 6 s. Everything else behaves as described above.

A blocking, single-caller, variable-length API is not something the rest of
the daemon can call. Wrap it.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.  Publish the latest scan.

    int    wifi_table_init(void);
    void   wifi_table_deinit(void);
    size_t wifi_get_snapshot(struct ApInfo *out, size_t max);

  Any number of threads must be able to ask for the most recent COMPLETE
  scan at any time. wifi_get_snapshot() must never block on the radio and
  must never hand back a list stitched together from two different scans, or
  a list whose count and contents disagree.

  The thing that makes this different from "publish the latest float": the
  published value is a VARIABLE-LENGTH COLLECTION — a count plus up to 40
  entries, about 1.6 KB. One atomic word cannot carry it, and copying it
  under a mutex makes every reader wait for every other reader.

  Before you code, decide between:
      (a) mutex + memcpy under the lock
      (b) two or three preallocated buffers + an atomic "current" index +
          a refcount (or seqlock-style validation)
      (c) RCU-style: malloc a new buffer, swap the pointer, free the old one
  and be able to say in one sentence why you rejected two of them.

Part 2.  Which APs are worth connecting to.

    void   wifi_table_observe(const struct ApInfo *aps, size_t n);
    size_t wifi_top_k(struct ApEntry *out, size_t k);
    int    wifi_lookup(const uint8_t bssid[6], struct ApEntry *out);
    size_t wifi_table_count(void);
    size_t wifi_table_dropped(void);

    struct ApEntry {
        uint8_t  bssid[6];
        char     ssid[33];
        float    rssi_smoothed;   /* EWMA, alpha = AP_EWMA_ALPHA = 1/4 */
        uint32_t times_seen;
        uint64_t last_seen_us;
    };

  Fold every scan into a table keyed by BSSID and answer two questions:
    - wifi_top_k: the k strongest APs by SMOOTHED rssi, restricted to APs
      seen within the last 5 minutes (AP_STALE_US), strongest first.
    - wifi_lookup: one BSSID, O(1) average.

  Constraints that pick the data structure for you:
    - the key is 6 raw bytes, not an int and not a string
    - k is small (1..16) and N is the whole table: do NOT sort all N
    - entries expire on a 5-minute window, and there is no timer thread
    - no malloc per AP if you can avoid it (embedded target)

  Raw RSSI from a single scan swings 5-10 dB from multipath, so the ranking
  must be built on the smoothed value, not the last sample. Say why an EWMA
  beats "keep the last value" and beats "average the last N".

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour (what the harness checks)

  Part 1
    - a snapshot is available immediately after init (init takes one scan
      synchronously) and is byte-identical to the scan the radio produced
    - NEVER torn: every AP in one scan carries the same generation tag in
      its `channel` field, and the harness memcmp's your whole snapshot
      against the exact bytes of that logged scan
    - variable length is honoured: an empty scan (0 APs) and a full scan
      (WIFI_MAX_APS) both come out correctly, and a small `max` is clamped
    - the snapshot keeps advancing while scans keep coming (>= 10 distinct
      generations observed in 2 s)

  Part 2
    - rssi_smoothed, times_seen, last_seen_us and ssid all match a
      brute-force replay of every scan the radio ever returned
    - top_k for k = 1, 3, 8, 40, 72 matches the reference ranking exactly,
      including the tie-break rule; k = 0 returns 0
    - TIE-BREAK RULE (documented, so the result is deterministic): equal
      rssi_smoothed -> smaller BSSID first (memcmp ascending). Two APs in
      the fake environment tie exactly, on purpose.
    - lookup hits for a known BSSID, misses for an unknown one
    - after the clock jumps past the 5-minute window: count == 0, lookup
      misses, top_k returns nothing
    - offered 1024 distinct APs into a 256-slot table: exactly 256 stored,
      768 counted as dropped, the stored ones still found, the dropped ones
      absent

  Both
    - 4 reader threads for 2 s against a live radio: no torn, invented or
      misordered value, and lookups actually hit
    - getter latency, measured alone: no call ever sits through a scan, and
      over-2 ms calls are under 0.5% (scheduler noise, not design)
    - after deinit the thread is gone: no further scan happens, and calling
      deinit twice is safe

  Last line is exactly "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S) FAILED".

